.TH SIM6809 1 2026-10-26 plm09 "plm09 Manual"
.SH NAME
sim6809 \- headless Motorola 6809 simulator
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sim6809
.RI [ options ]
.I prog.s19
.br
.B sim6809 \-\-os9
.RB [ \-\-load
.IR addr ]
.RB [ \-\-data
.IR addr ]
.RB [ \-\-param
.IR string ]
.RI [ options ]
.I module
.br
.B sim6809 \-\-flex
.RI [ options ]
.I prog.cmd
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sim6809
runs a 6809 program without a display: the complete documented MC6809
instruction set, with interrupts, and a 16-byte I/O block for character
I/O and the exit status.
It loads Motorola S-records (S1 and S9; S0 and S5 are ignored) and Intel
HEX records (types 00 and 01), and starts at the S9 record's address,
.BR \-\-entry ,
or with
.BR \-\-reset ,
the reset vector.
Memory is 64 KB of RAM, filled with $A5 at the start.
.PP
It was written to test
.BR plmc (1)'s
output, and can also stand in for OS-9 (running program modules), FLEX
(running .CMD files), and small boards, with their own ROMs (an MC6850
ACIA, an FT245RL USB FIFO, software interrupts and ROM).
.SH OPTIONS
Numbers are decimal unless they start with
.B 0x
or
.BR $ .
.TP
.B \-t
Trace each instruction to standard error before it runs: its address,
bytes, disassembly and the registers.
.TP
.BI \-n " n"
Stop after
.I n
instructions (default 100000000).
.TP
.BI \-\-io " base"
Put the I/O block at
.I base
(default $FF00).
.TP
.BI \-\-entry " addr"
Start at
.IR addr .
.TP
.B \-\-reset
Start at the address in the reset vector ($FFFE), as the 6809 does; for
ROM images.
.TP
.BI \-\-rom " addr"
Memory from
.I addr
to $FFFF is ROM: writes to it are ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-zero
Clear memory instead of filling it with $A5.
.TP
.B \-\-os9
The file is an OS-9 program module.
Set up a process as F$Fork does, emulate the system calls F$Exit,
I$Read, I$ReadLn, I$Write and I$WritLn, and enforce memory protection:
code must stay in the module, and writes in the data area.
.TP
.BI \-\-load " addr"
With
.BR \-\-os9 :
load the module at
.I addr
(default $2000).
.TP
.BI \-\-data " addr"
With
.BR \-\-os9 :
put the data area at
.IR addr ,
a multiple of 256 (default $8000).
.TP
.BI \-\-param " string"
With
.BR \-\-os9 :
the parameter string (default empty).
.TP
.B \-\-flex
The file is a FLEX binary (.CMD).
Emulate the FLEX DOS entry points that programs call (the terminal and
number routines; WARMS ends the run, with B as the exit status) and
protect FLEX's memory ($C700\-$DFFF).
.TP
.BI \-\-acia " addr"
An MC6850 ACIA with its status at
.I addr
and data at
.IR addr +1,
on standard input and output.
A character is offered once the program has polled the status 32 times
since its last data access, as a person types after a prompt.
At the end of input it reads 0.
.TP
.B \-\-stop\-at\-end
With
.BR \-\-acia :
a program that waits for input after its end ends the run, with exit
status 0, instead of reading 0.
.TP
.BI \-\-ft245 " addr"
An FT245RL USB FIFO at
.IR addr ,
on standard input and output, asserting FIRQ while input is waiting.
Waiting (SYNC or CWAI) after the end of input ends the run with exit
status 0.
.TP
.B \-\-swi
Execute SWI, SWI2 and SWI3 through their vectors instead of stopping.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH I/O BLOCK
Offsets from the base ($FF00):
.TP
.B +0
write: print the character
.TP
.B +1
read: the next character of standard input, 0 at its end
.TP
.B +2
write: stop, with the byte as the exit status
.TP
.B +4, +5
write: print the 16-bit value (high byte at +4) in decimal and a newline,
on the write to +5
.TP
.B +6
write: assert interrupt lines (1 IRQ, 2 FIRQ, 4 NMI); read: the asserted
lines
.TP
.B +7
write: release lines
.TP
.B +8, +9
write: the timer period in instructions, started by the write to +9 (0
stops it)
.TP
.B +10
write: the lines the timer asserts (default IRQ)
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0\-255
The value the program wrote to the exit port (or B, for OS-9's F$Exit
and FLEX's WARMS).
.TP
.B 124
The instruction limit was reached.
.TP
.B 125
An illegal opcode or indexed postbyte, SWI without
.BR \-\-swi ,
an unsupported OS-9 call or FLEX entry point, or a memory protection
violation.
The registers are printed to standard error.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error, or a file that couldn't be loaded.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a program, with input from a file, and trace it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sim6809 \-t prog.s19 < input.txt 2> trace.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run Grant Searle's 6809 SBC BASIC ROM, an Intel HEX file, on the terminal:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sim6809 \-\-acia 0xA000 \-\-io 0x9000 \-\-rom 0xC000 \-\-reset ExBasROM.hex
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR plmc (1),
.BR srec2flex (1)
.PP
The sim6809 manual,
.I docs/sim6809.pdf
in the plm09 source tree, describes the CPU, the devices, the OS-9 and
FLEX modes and tracing in detail.
